      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The sudden, aggressive sting is due to the nature of the volatile compounds and the speed of the reaction in the moist environment of the eye. When the onion cells are ruptured, the alliinase enzyme is suddenly exposed to its substrate, leading to a rapid, almost instantaneous burst of gaseous sulfur compounds. These molecules are highly reactive, and when they encounter the water on your eye surface, the hydrolysis to form sulfuric acid happens extremely quickly. It's not a slow, steady burn, but a sudden, sharp chemical assault, much like a sudden, unexpected burst of a highly volatile, acidic vapor. This rapid release and immediate acid formation is what gives it that \"mischievous\" punch.",
